  <h2 id="pre-requisites">1. Pre-requisites</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Tool</th>
        <th>Required Version / Notes</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>R</td>
        <td>Any recent version. R is used for setup chunks, plots, and rendered examples.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>RStudio</td>
        <td>Recommended for editing, previewing, rendering, and using Git.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Quarto</td>
        <td>Required for rendering <code>.qmd</code> files into <code>.html</code>.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>GitHub access</td>
        <td>Required to push source files and rendered website output.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>R packages</td>
        <td>Usually <code>ggplot2</code> and <code>dplyr</code>, plus any package listed in a chapter YAML.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Browser</td>
        <td>Chrome or Firefox recommended for checking WebR, quizzes, and interactive widgets.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="introduction">2. Introduction</h2>

  <p>This project is a Quarto slide website for <strong>STA258: Statistics with Applied Probability</strong> at the University of Toronto Mississauga.</p>

  <p>The project is not a bookdown book. It is organized around <strong>chapter landing pages</strong> and <strong>chapter slide decks</strong>. The source files are written as <code>.qmd</code> files and rendered into <code>.html</code> files that students access through the course website.</p>

  <p>Key features of this slides project:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Homepage</strong> where students access chapter pages.</li>
    <li><strong>Chapter landing pages</strong> inside <code>Chapter Landing Pages/</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>Reveal.js slide decks</strong> inside <code>Chapter Slides/</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>WebR code blocks</strong> so students can run R in the browser.</li>
    <li><strong>Quiz plugin slides</strong> with correct-answer explanations.</li>
    <li><strong>Static R plots</strong> placed beside examples.</li>
    <li><strong>Interactive JavaScript widgets</strong>, especially in Chapter 4.</li>
    <li><strong>PDF views</strong> created from slide links using <code>?print-pdf</code>.</li>
    <li><strong>GitHub Pages output</strong> served from the <code>docs/</code> folder.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="how-to-use">3. How to Use This Help File</h2>

  <p>This document is intended for future maintainers, TAs, students, and professors who need to update the STA258 slides website or troubleshoot problems.</p>

  <p>For most updates, use this workflow:</p>

  <ol>
    <li><strong>Edit the source <code>.qmd</code> file.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Render locally</strong> using Quarto.</li>
    <li><strong>Open the rendered HTML file</strong> and check the result in the browser.</li>
    <li><strong>Test WebR, quizzes, plots, links, and interactive widgets.</strong></li>
    <li><strong>Commit both the source file and rendered output</strong> if the website uses <code>docs/</code> for GitHub Pages.</li>
    <li><strong>Push to GitHub</strong> and check the live website.</li>
  </ol>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Main rule:</strong> Edit source files first, render second, check the output third, and push only after confirming the rendered website works.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="folder-structure">4. Folder Structure</h2>

  <p>The current repository is organized around separate folders for chapter landing pages and chapter slide decks.</p>

  <pre><code>STA258_Slides/
  index.qmd
  _quarto.yml
  styles.css
  README.md
  LICENSE
  .nojekyll

  Chapter Landing Pages/
    chapter1.qmd
    chapter2.qmd
    chapter3.qmd
    chapter4.qmd

  Chapter Slides/
    chapter1-slides.qmd
    chapter2-slides.qmd
    chapter3-slides.qmd
    chapter4-slides.qmd

  Lectures/
  _extensions/
  images/
  docs/</code></pre>

  <div class="warning">
    <p><strong>Important:</strong> The <code>docs/</code> folder contains rendered output. Do not manually edit files inside <code>docs/</code>; edit the source <code>.qmd</code> files and re-render.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Keep <code>.nojekyll</code> in the repository.</strong> This helps GitHub Pages serve folders such as <code>_extensions/</code> correctly instead of ignoring underscore-prefixed folders.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="files">5. Project Files and Where to Edit</h2>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>If you want to...</th>
        <th>Edit this file</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Add or change homepage text, chapter cards, release information, or resource links</td>
        <td><code>index.qmd</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Add or change a chapter overview page</td>
        <td><code>Chapter Landing Pages/chapterX.qmd</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Add or change lecture slides, examples, plots, quizzes, WebR, or interactive widgets</td>
        <td><code>Chapter Slides/chapterX-slides.qmd</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Change shared website page colours, cards, buttons, spacing, or layout</td>
        <td><code>styles.css</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Change project-wide Quarto settings</td>
        <td><code>_quarto.yml</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Fix rendered website output</td>
        <td>Edit the source <code>.qmd</code>, then re-render. Do not manually patch <code>docs/</code>.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="file-card">
    <h3><code>index.qmd</code></h3>
    <p>The main website landing page where students access chapters and resources.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="file-card">
    <h3><code>Chapter Landing Pages/chapterX.qmd</code></h3>
    <p>Chapter landing pages. These should provide chapter descriptions and links to the slide deck and PDF view.</p>
  </div>

  <div class="file-card">
    <h3><code>Chapter Slides/chapterX-slides.qmd</code></h3>
    <p>The actual slide decks. These contain lecture content, CSS, WebR chunks, R plots, quiz slides, in-class examples, and interactive JavaScript widgets.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="slide-structure">6. Slide Deck Structure</h2>

  <p>Each chapter slide deck should follow the same teaching and visual structure so the course feels consistent.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Element</th>
        <th>Standard for this project</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Format</td>
        <td>Use <code>live-revealjs</code> with slide numbers, chalkboard, MathJax, WebR, and consistent dimensions.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Theme</td>
        <td>Use the STA258 teal, blue, yellow, grey, and orange colour system.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Opening</td>
        <td>Title slide, course name, university name, and cat mascot image.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Roadmap</td>
        <td>Include a chapter roadmap near the beginning.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Content flow</td>
        <td>Definition → intuition → example → plot → R check → quiz.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Practice</td>
        <td>Use task boxes, WebR blocks, quizzes, and in-class examples.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Ending</td>
        <td>Chapter summary, up-next slide, and back-to-homepage button.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>A recommended slide deck flow is:</p>

  <pre><code>Title slide
Chapter roadmap

Main concept 1
Definition / intuition
Worked example
Plot or visual
Check in R
Quiz question

Main concept 2
Definition / intuition
Worked example
Plot or visual
Check in R
Quiz question

In-class examples
Chapter summary
Up next
Back to Homepage button</code></pre>

  <h2 id="chapter4-standard">7. Current Chapter 4 Slide Standard</h2>

  <p>The latest Chapter 4 slide deck is the current standard for future slide work. It uses <code>live-revealjs</code>, the quiz plugin, WebR, static R plots, scrollable slides, and custom JavaScript widgets.</p>

  <h3>YAML structure</h3>

  <pre><code>---
pagetitle: "Chapter 4: Foundations of Inference - Slides"
engine: knitr
format:
  live-revealjs:
    theme: simple
    transition: slide
    slide-number: true
    toc: false
    chalkboard: true
    footer: "STA258 | Chapter 4"
    pdf-separate-fragments: false
    html-math-method: mathjax
    code-copy: true
    code-fold: false
    incremental: false
    width: 1280
    height: 720
    margin: 0.055
    center: false
revealjs-plugins:
  - quiz
webr:
  packages:
    - ggplot2
    - dplyr
live:
  show-solutions: true
---</code></pre>

  <p>The important current detail is the quiz plugin:</p>

  <pre><code>revealjs-plugins:
  - quiz</code></pre>

  <p>Because this plugin is used, quiz slides should follow the plugin-style quiz format instead of manual HTML button quizzes.</p>

  <h3>Current Chapter 4 paths</h3>

  <pre><code>{{&lt; include ../_extensions/r-wasm/live/_knitr.qmd &gt;}}

![](../images/cat.png){width="245" fig-alt="Course mascot"}

&lt;a href="../index.html" class="home-button"&gt;Back to Homepage&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Path note:</strong> These examples use <code>../</code>, which means “go up one folder.” This is correct for slide files inside <code>Chapter Slides/</code> because they need to access files from the project root.</p>
  </div>

  <h3>Current Chapter 4 features to preserve</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>STA258 CSS theme inside the slide deck.</li>
    <li><code>.defbox</code>, <code>.examplebox</code>, <code>.thinkbox</code>, <code>.taskbox</code>, <code>.warnbox</code>, and <code>.stat-card</code> styles.</li>
    <li>Scrollable slides using <code>{.scrollable}</code>.</li>
    <li>Quiz slides using <code>{.quiz-question}</code>.</li>
    <li>Interactive Empirical Rule and t-distribution widgets using HTML, canvas, sliders, and JavaScript.</li>
    <li>In-class example slides with a task box on the left and plot on the right.</li>
    <li>WebR “Check in R” slides after examples.</li>
    <li>Back-to-homepage button using <code>../index.html</code>.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="paths">8. File Paths and ../ Rules</h2>

  <p>Many problems in this slide project come from file paths. Always check where the current file is located before writing links.</p>

  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Situation</th>
        <th>Path pattern</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>Root-level file, such as <code>index.qmd</code></td>
        <td><code>images/cat.png</code>, <code>Chapter Landing Pages/chapter4.html</code>, <code>Chapter Slides/chapter4-slides.html</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Landing page inside <code>Chapter Landing Pages/</code></td>
        <td><code>../index.html</code>, <code>../Chapter Slides/chapter4-slides.html</code>, <code>../images/cat.png</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Slide file inside <code>Chapter Slides/</code></td>
        <td><code>../index.html</code>, <code>../images/cat.png</code>, <code>../_extensions/r-wasm/live/_knitr.qmd</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Image from a root-level file</td>
        <td><code>images/cat.png</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td>Image from a slide or landing page folder</td>
        <td><code>../images/cat.png</code></td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <div class="warning">
    <p><strong>Rule:</strong> If an image, homepage button, WebR include, or slide link does not work, first check whether the path needs <code>../</code>.</p>
  </div>

  <p>Examples of dataset paths in R:</p>

  <pre><code>scores &lt;- read.csv("../data/scores.csv")
penguins &lt;- read.csv("../data/penguins.csv")</code></pre>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Case matters on GitHub Pages.</strong> <code>cat.png</code>, <code>Cat.png</code>, and <code>CAT.png</code> may be treated as different files. Match the file name exactly.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="examples">9. In-Class Examples and Plots</h2>

  <p>In-class examples should usually appear as two slides:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>A prompt slide with a <code>taskbox</code> and a plot beside it.</li>
    <li>A WebR check slide where students can run the calculation.</li>
  </ol>

  <h3>Recommended structure</h3>

  <pre><code>---

## In-Class Example: Example Title {.scrollable}

:::: {.two-col-wide}
::: {.col}
::: {.taskbox}
&lt;span class="label"&gt;In-Class Example&lt;/span&gt;

Question text goes here.

**(a)** First task.

**(b)** Second task.

**(c)** Interpretation question.
:::
:::

::: {.col}
```{r}
#| fig-width: 5.8
#| fig-height: 4
#| fig-align: center
#| echo: false

# Plot code goes here
```
:::
::::

---

## Example Title - Check in R

```{webr}
#| autorun: false

# Student-check code goes here
```</code></pre>

  <h3>Chapter 3 example topics</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Mean versus median.</li>
    <li>Range versus IQR.</li>
    <li>Histograms and skewness.</li>
    <li>Boxplots and outliers.</li>
    <li>Comparing two groups.</li>
    <li>Same mean but different spread.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Chapter 4 example topics</h3>

  <ul>
    <li>Parameter versus statistic.</li>
    <li>Sampling variability.</li>
    <li>Normal probabilities and standardizing.</li>
    <li>Chi-square, t, and F probability calculations.</li>
    <li>Central Limit Theorem simulations.</li>
    <li>Law of Large Numbers simulations.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="webr">10. WebR in Slides</h2>

  <p>WebR lets students run R code directly inside the browser.</p>

  <p>A basic WebR chunk looks like this:</p>

  <pre><code>```{webr}
#| autorun: false

x &lt;- c(2, 4, 6, 8)
mean(x)
sd(x)
```</code></pre>

  <p>If a WebR chunk uses a package, list the package in the YAML:</p>

  <pre><code>webr:
  packages:
    - ggplot2
    - dplyr</code></pre>

  <p>Then load the package in the relevant chunk if needed:</p>

  <pre><code>library(ggplot2)
library(dplyr)</code></pre>

  <p>If using live exercise solutions, make sure the exercise name and solution name match exactly:</p>

  <pre><code>```{webr}
#| exercise: sample-mean
#| autorun: false

x &lt;- c(2, 4, 6, 8)
mean(x)
```

::: {.solution exercise="sample-mean"}
```{webr}
x &lt;- c(2, 4, 6, 8)
mean(x)
```
:::</code></pre>

  <div class="warning">
    <p><strong>Do not use <code>install.packages()</code> inside WebR chunks.</strong> Packages should be declared in the YAML instead.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="quizzes">11. Quiz Slides</h2>

  <p>The current Chapter 4 uses the reveal.js quiz plugin.</p>

  <p>Make sure the YAML includes:</p>

  <pre><code>revealjs-plugins:
  - quiz</code></pre>

  <p>Use this structure for quiz slides:</p>

  <pre><code>---

##  {.quiz-question}

:::: {.callout-note icon="false"}
## Example

Question text goes here.
::::

- Wrong option
- [Correct option]{.correct data-explanation="Explanation goes here."}
- Wrong option
- Wrong option</code></pre>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Rule:</strong> Keep quiz questions short. Each quiz should test one idea only. The explanation should explain why the correct answer is correct.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="widgets">12. Interactive JavaScript Widgets</h2>

  <p>Chapter 4 includes interactive JavaScript widgets, such as the Empirical Rule explorer and the t-distribution tail comparison.</p>

  <p>These widgets usually have two parts:</p>

  <ol>
    <li>An HTML block containing sliders, labels, and a <code>&lt;canvas&gt;</code>.</li>
    <li>A <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block that updates the drawing.</li>
  </ol>

  <pre><code>&lt;div id="example-widget"&gt;
  &lt;input type="range"&gt;
  &lt;canvas id="example-canvas"&gt;&lt;/canvas&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;script&gt;
window.exampleUpdate = function () {
  // JavaScript drawing code goes here
};
&lt;/script&gt;</code></pre>

  <div class="warning">
    <p><strong>Do not delete the <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block.</strong> The sliders and canvas may still appear, but the widget will not update without the JavaScript.</p>
  </div>

  <p>If a widget does not work, check:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The HTML <code>id</code> names match the JavaScript.</li>
    <li>The slider calls the correct function using <code>oninput</code>.</li>
    <li>The <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block was not deleted.</li>
    <li>The same IDs are not duplicated elsewhere on the page.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="rendering">13. Rendering, docs, and Final Checks</h2>

  <p>After editing a source file, render it to create the updated HTML output.</p>

  <p>To render the whole website:</p>

  <pre><code>quarto render</code></pre>

  <p>To render only one slide deck:</p>

  <pre><code>quarto render "Chapter Slides/chapter4-slides.qmd"</code></pre>

  <p>The <code>docs/</code> folder contains the rendered website output that GitHub Pages serves.</p>

  <p>The correct workflow is:</p>

  <pre><code>Edit source .qmd
Render with Quarto
Quarto updates docs/
Check the rendered HTML
Commit source and docs output
Push changes to GitHub</code></pre>

  <p>Before pushing changes, check:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The homepage opens correctly.</li>
    <li>Chapter cards link to the correct pages.</li>
    <li>Chapter landing pages link to the correct slide decks.</li>
    <li>Slide decks open correctly.</li>
    <li>PDF buttons open the correct print view.</li>
    <li>Plots display correctly.</li>
    <li>WebR chunks load and run.</li>
    <li>Quiz answer checking works.</li>
    <li>Interactive widgets respond to sliders.</li>
    <li>Images load.</li>
    <li>The back-to-homepage button works.</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="warning">
    <p><strong>Never manually edit files inside <code>docs/</code>.</strong> Any manual edits may be overwritten the next time Quarto renders the website.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="github">14. GitHub Workflow and Common Commands</h2>

  <p>Because this repository has folders with spaces in their names, use quotation marks around file paths in Git commands.</p>

  <p>A safe Git workflow is:</p>

  <pre><code>git status
git add "path/to/file"
git status
git commit -m "Describe the change"
git push</code></pre>

  <p>Use <code>git status</code> before adding files to see what changed. Use it again after adding files to confirm only the intended files are staged.</p>

  <p>Example for only updating Chapter 4 slides:</p>

  <pre><code>git status
git add "Chapter Slides/chapter4-slides.qmd"
git status
git commit -m "Update Chapter 4 slides"
git push</code></pre>

  <p>If rendering changes the output in <code>docs/</code>, also add the matching rendered files shown by <code>git status</code>. Only add the exact <code>docs/</code> files that changed.</p>

  <pre><code>git add "docs/Chapter Slides/chapter4-slides.html"</code></pre>

  <h3>Other useful commands</h3>

  <pre><code>quarto preview
quarto render
quarto render "Chapter Slides/chapter4-slides.qmd"
quarto clean
quarto clean --all</code></pre>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Important:</strong> If you only changed one chapter, do not stage the entire repository unless you have checked that the other changed files are supposed to be included.</p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="errors">15. Common Slide Errors and Known Issues</h2>

  <h3>Broken links</h3>

  <p>Check that links point to rendered <code>.html</code> files, not source <code>.qmd</code> files.</p>

  <pre><code>Correct:
&lt;a href="../Chapter Slides/chapter4-slides.html"&gt;Open Chapter 4 Slides&lt;/a&gt;

Incorrect:
&lt;a href="../Chapter Slides/chapter4-slides.qmd"&gt;Open Chapter 4 Slides&lt;/a&gt;</code></pre>

  <h3>PDF button not working</h3>

  <p>Make sure the PDF button points to the rendered slide deck with <code>?print-pdf</code>.</p>

  <pre><code>Correct:
../Chapter Slides/chapter4-slides.html?print-pdf

Incorrect:
../Chapter Landing Pages/chapter4.html?print-pdf</code></pre>

  <h3>Image not loading</h3>

  <p>Check the path and capitalization.</p>

  <pre><code>![](images/cat.png)
![](../images/cat.png)</code></pre>

  <h3>WebR not working</h3>

  <p>Check:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The chunk uses <code>{webr}</code>.</li>
    <li>The WebR helper include exists.</li>
    <li>Required packages are listed in the YAML.</li>
    <li>The code does not use <code>install.packages()</code>.</li>
    <li>The file path to the WebR extension is correct.</li>
  </ul>

  <pre><code>{{&lt; include ../_extensions/r-wasm/live/_knitr.qmd &gt;}}</code></pre>

  <h3>Quiz not working</h3>

  <p>Check:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The YAML includes <code>revealjs-plugins: - quiz</code>.</li>
    <li>The slide has <code>{.quiz-question}</code>.</li>
    <li>The correct answer uses <code>{.correct data-explanation="..."}</code>.</li>
    <li>The options are written as a plain Markdown list.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Interactive widget not working</h3>

  <p>Check:</p>

  <ul>
    <li>The HTML IDs match the JavaScript IDs.</li>
    <li>The slider calls the correct update function using <code>oninput</code>.</li>
    <li>The <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> block is still present.</li>
    <li>The same widget IDs were not duplicated elsewhere.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Rendering error</h3>

  <p>Common causes include broken YAML indentation, missing closing backticks, broken HTML tags, wrong file names, wrong image paths, package errors, or trying to render the wrong file.</p>

  <div class="note">
    <p><strong>Fix the first error first.</strong> Later errors are often caused by the first one.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>Final Note</h2>

  <p>The main rule for maintaining this project is simple: edit the source <code>.qmd</code> files, render them, check the rendered website, and only then push to GitHub.</p>

  <p>Keep the slide structure consistent so students experience the website as one polished course resource rather than separate disconnected files.</p>

